Usage: cp [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Copy SOURCE to DEST, or multiple SOURCE(s) to DIRECTORY.

In the first form, copy SOURCE to DEST.
In the second form, copy each SOURCE to DIRECTORY.

OPTIONS:
      --keep-directory-symlink  follow existing symlink to directory
  -a, --archive                 same as -dR --preserve=all
  -b                            like --backup but does not accept an argument
      --backup[=ARG]            make a backup of each existing destination file
  -d                            same as --no-dereference --preserve=links
  -f, --force                   if an existing destination file cannot be
                                opened, remove it and try again
  -i, --interactive             prompt before overwrite
  -H                            follow command-line symbolic links in SOURCE
  -l, --link                    hard link files instead of copying
  -L, --dereference             always follow symbolic links in SOURCE
  -n, --no-clobber              do not overwrite an existing file and do not
                                fail
  -P, --no-dereference          never follow symbolic links in SOURCE
  -p                            same as --preserve=mode,ownership,timestamps
  -R, --recursive               copy directories recursively
  -r, --recursive               copy directories recursively
  -s, --symbolic-link           make symbolic links instead of copying
  -S, --suffix ARG              override the usual backup suffix
  -t, --target-directory ARG    copy all SOURCE arguments into DIRECTORY
  -T, --no-target-directory     treat DEST as a normal file
      --strip-trailing-slashes  remove any trailing slashes from each SOURCE
                                argument
  -u, --update[=ARG]            equivalent to --update[=older]
  -v, --verbose                 explain what is being done
      --debug                   explain how a file is copied; implies --verbose
  -g, --progress-bar            display a progress bar while copying
  -x, --one-file-system         stay on this file system
  -Z                            set SELinux security context of destination file
                                to default type
      --remove-destination      remove each existing destination file before
                                attempting to open it
      --attributes-only         don't copy the file data, just the attributes
      --parents                 use full source file name under DIRECTORY
      --parent                  use full source file name under DIRECTORY
      --path                    use full source file name under DIRECTORY
      --sparse ARG              control creation of sparse files
      --reflink[=ARG]           control clone/CoW copies
      --preserve ARG            preserve the specified attributes
      --no-preserve ARG         don't preserve the specified attributes
      --copy-contents           copy contents of special files when recursive
  -c, --context                 set SELinux security context of destination file
                                to default type
      --help                    display this help and exit
  -V, --version                 output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
